The project brings together several scientific and computational components:

Scientific data:

- NASA LRO LOLA elevation datasets.
- NASA PDS Geosciences Node.
- NASA NAIF SPICE kernels.
- NASA JPL Horizons API.

Computational methods:

- Digital Elevation Model processing.
- Ray-marching terrain analysis.
- Spherical lunar curvature correction.
- Astronomical ephemeris calculations.
- Solar illumination analysis.
- Line-of-sight visibility assessment.
- Automated numerical validation.

User interface:

- Interactive web dashboard.
- Comparative lunar site analysis.
- Visualization of illumination and Earth visibility results.

10. Limitations and Challenges

During the development of SOL Planner, we identified several technical limitations that influence the accuracy and scope of the simulations.

10.1. DEM Spatial Resolution

The LOLA dataset used in the project has an approximate spatial resolution of 20 meters per pixel.

Although useful for regional terrain analysis, this resolution may not represent every small geographical feature.

Consequently, some narrow obstacles, local slopes, and small-scale terrain variations may not be captured.

10.2. Maximum Analysis Distance

Our current ray-marching implementation analyzes terrain over distances of up to 70 km.

Geographical obstacles beyond this range are not necessarily represented in the current visibility assessment.

10.3. Dependence on Data Quality

The quality of the results depends on the availability, coverage, resolution, and accuracy of the underlying lunar elevation data.

Errors or missing information in the DEM may affect terrain-obstruction calculations.

10.4. Communication System Limitations

Geometrical visibility between a lunar location and Earth does not automatically guarantee successful radio communication.

Actual communication also depends on antenna orientation, spacecraft hardware, transmission power, receiver sensitivity, link budget, and other operational conditions.

The current project focuses on geometric direct visibility rather than complete radio-frequency link-budget simulation.

10.5. Simulation Period

The presented comparison uses a simulated observation window of 30 days.

Results obtained for this period should not automatically be interpreted as permanent environmental characteristics or as predictions for every future mission date.

10.6. Validation Scope

The JPL Horizons validation evaluates astronomical position calculations.

Further testing with additional lunar locations, longer observation periods, independent terrain datasets, and operational communication parameters would strengthen the overall validation of the platform.

11. Benefits and Expected Impact

SOL Planner aims to contribute to lunar mission planning through several practical benefits.

Improved Mission Safety

By identifying periods of darkness and potential communication interruptions, the platform can help mission planners anticipate environmental risks.

Better Energy Planning

The identification of locations with favorable solar illumination can support the selection of sites for solar-powered infrastructure.

More Reliable Communication Planning

Predicting direct Earth visibility windows can help mission operators plan communication schedules and identify potential terrain-related interruptions.

Improved Landing Site Assessment

Comparing lunar topography, solar illumination, and Earth visibility can support more informed site-selection decisions.

Support for Scientific Exploration

Identifying permanently shadowed regions can help guide scientific investigations into locations that may contain water ice and other valuable resources.

Interdisciplinary Innovation

The project demonstrates how students from different academic backgrounds can apply computational science, mathematical reasoning, and publicly available space-agency data to address complex scientific challenges.

12. Our Vision

Our long-term vision is to develop SOL Planner into a more comprehensive lunar environmental analysis and mission-support platform.

Future improvements could include:

- Higher-resolution lunar terrain datasets.
- Expanded geographical coverage.
- Longer simulation periods.
- More detailed rover and lander energy models.
- Radio-frequency communication link-budget analysis.
- Integration of additional lunar environmental datasets.
- Improved visualization and mission-planning capabilities.

Ultimately, we aim to contribute to the development of tools that make lunar exploration safer, more efficient, and more sustainable.

